  "body": "Noted by the high-tier review of PR #1043 (review comment on that PR, head 1a6d4017). The law is on main (#369); PR #1043 is what first lets a user reach it.\n\n**What.** `Reach::radii` reads a lathe holder body point at radius `|X + x|`, where `X` is the tool tip's signed radial position and `x` the body point's radial offset in the turning tool frame. The UI and MCP wording says \"+x points away from the spindle axis\". Both are true only for a tip at X >= 0.\n\n- Facing a little past centre (tip at a small negative X): `X + x` is still the body's real position, so the reading is right; only the wording is loose.\n- A tool that really sits on the other side of the axis (a rear turret or gang position programmed at negative X), if any turning path can carry one: the body would be read mirrored through the axis, so a holder that hits could be judged clear.\n\n**Not known.** Whether any turning operation or machine definition today can put a tip at a negative X other than the facing overrun. If none can, this is a wording fix plus a guard; if one can, it is a collision-law defect and should be re-rated P0.\n\nDone means: it is stated (with a test) which signed-X cases the turning generators can produce; the law either handles a tip on the negative side correctly or refuses it by name; the UI/MCP wording says what +x means for both cases.\n",
